.TH RZ 1 OMEN
.SH NAME
rx, rb, rz \- XMODEM, YMODEM, ZMODEM (Batch) file receive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rz
.RB [\- "\ +abeOpqRtuUvy" ]
.br
.B rb
.RB [\- "\ +abqRtuUvy" ]
.br
.B rx
.RB [\- "\ abceqRtuUv" ]
.I file
.br
.RB [ \- ][ v ] rzCOMMAND
.SH DESCRIPTION
This program uses error correcting protocols to receive
files over a dial-in serial port from a variety of programs running under
PC-DOS, CP/M,
.SM Unix,
and other operating systems.
It is invoked from a shell prompt
manually, or automatically as a result of an
"sz file ..." command given to the calling program.

.B Rz
(Receive ZMODEM)
receives files with the ZMODEM batch protocol.
Pathnames are supplied by the sending program,
and directories are made if necessary (and possible).
Normally, the
"rz" command is automatically issued by the calling ZMODEM program,
but some defective ZMODEM implementations may require starting
.I rz
the old fashioned way.


.B Rb
receives file(s) with YMODEM,
accepting either standard 128 byte sectors or
1024 byte sectors
(lsb
.B -k
option).
The user should determine when
the 1024 byte block length
actually improves throughput without causing lost data
or even system crashes.

If True YMODEM (Omen Technology trademark) file information (file length, etc.)
is received,
the file length controls the number of bytes written to
the output dataset,
and the modify time and file mode
(iff non zero)
are set accordingly.

If no True YMODEM file information is received,
slashes in the pathname are changed to underscore,
and any trailing period in the pathname is eliminated.
This conversion is useful for files received from CP/M systems.
With YMODEM, each file name is converted to lower case
unless it contains one or more lower case letters.


.B Rx
receives a single
.I file
with XMODEM or XMODEM-1k protocol.
The user should determine when
the 1024 byte block length
actually improves throughput without causing problems.
The user must supply the file name to both sending and receiving programs.
Up to 1023 garbage characters may be added to the received file.


.B Rz
may be invoked as
.B rzCOMMAND
(with an optional leading \- as generated by login(1)).
For each received file,
.I rz
will pipe the file to ``COMMAND filename''
where filename is the name of the transmitted file
with the file contents as standard input.

Each file transfer is acknowledged when COMMAND exits with 0 status.
A non zero exit status terminates transfers.

A typical use for this form is
.I rzrmail
which calls rmail(1)
to post mail to the user specified by the transmitted file name.
For example, sending the file "caf" from a PC-DOS system to
.I rzrmail
on a
.SM Unix
system
would result in the contents of the DOS file "caf" being mailed to user "caf".

On some
.SM Unix
systems, the login directory must contain a link to
COMMAND as login sets SHELL=rsh which disallows absolute
pathnames.
If invoked with a leading ``v'',
.I rz
will be verbose (see 
.B v
option).
The following entry works for
.SM Unix
SYS III/V:
.ce
rzrmail::5:1::/bin:/usr/local/rzrmail
If the SHELL environment variable includes
.I "rsh"
,
.I "rbash"
or
.I "rksh"
(restricted shell),
.I rz
will not accept absolute pathnames
or references to a parent directory,
will not modify an existing file, and
removes any files received in error.

If
.B rz
is invoked with stdout and stderr to different datasets,
Verbose is set to 2, causing frame by frame progress reports
to stderr.
This may be disabled with the
.B q
option.

.SH OPTIONS
The meanings of the available options are:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP
.B "-+, --append"
append received data to an existing file (ZMODEM, ASCII only).
.TP
.B "-a, --ascii"
Convert files to
.SM Unix
conventions by stripping carriage returns and all characters
beginning with the first Control Z (CP/M end of file).

Deprecated: this uses the ZCNL file conversion option, which current
ZMODEM documentation marks as historic and recommends against. ASCII
conversion also makes a transfer impossible to resume. It is kept for
compatibility with 40 year old peers, but new setups should transfer
in binary mode and convert locally if needed.

Note: for compatibility with old senders, a file mode sent with the
file metadata that includes file type bits (the historical full
.BR st_mode )
silently forces binary transfer for that file, overriding this
option, and disables the lower-casing of received file names.
This legacy behavior is deprecated as well and will likely be removed
in a future release.
.TP
.B "-A, --segmentsize NNNN"
Tell the sender to turn on `Segmented Streaming' with a buffer / window
of NNNN bytes. If the sender supports it, it will ask for an ZACK packet
every NNNN bytes. This somewhat limits throughput on perfect lines, and
can vastly improve performance on faulty lines.

NNNN can range from 0 to 65535 (this is a protocol limitation). '0', the
default, tells the sender that the receiver does not wish to use 
segmentation. Note that `lsz', the sending program of this package, does
then use its own default of 16kb.

Support for segmented streaming is not universal.
.TP
.B "-b, --binary"
Binary
(tell it like it is)
file transfer override.
.TP
.B "-B NUMBER, --bufsize NUMBER"
Buffer 
.B NUMBER
bytes before writing to disk. Default is 32768, which should be enough
for most situations. If you have a slow machine or a bad disk interface
or suffer from other hardware problems you might want to increase
the buffersize.
.TP
.B "-c, --with-crc"
XMODEM only. Use 16 bit CRC (normally a one byte checksum is used).
.TP
.B "-D, --null"
Output file data to /dev/null; for testing.
(Unix only)
.TP
.B "--delay-startup N"
Wait 
.B N
seconds before doing anything.
.TP
.B "-e, --escape"
Force sender to escape all control characters;
normally XON, XOFF, DLE, CR-@-CR, and Ctrl-X are escaped.
.TP
.B "-E, --rename"
Rename incoming file if target filename already exists. The new file
name will have a dot and a number (0..999) appended.
(ZMODEM and YMODEM only)
.TP
.B "-h, --help"
give help screen.
.TP
.B "-H, --crc-check"
(ZMODEM only; not usable with YMODEM, which lacks the required protocol
exchange) Accept and transfer files if the file lengths and/or the CRCs
differ.
.TP
.B "-J FILE, --journal FILE"
Append a transfer-statistics line to
.B FILE
for each file received.
The line consists of key=value pairs including timestamp, direction
(dir=rx), filename, status, byte counts, duration, and throughput.
The file is created if it does not exist and appended to on each
invocation.
.TP
.B "-m N, --min-bps N"
Stop transmission if BPS-Rate (Bytes Per Second) falls below N for a
certain time (see --min-bps-time option).
The check is per file: the timer resets at the start of each file.
.TP
.B "-M N, --min-bps-time"
Used together with --min-bps. Default is 120 (seconds).
The timeout is per file: a slow transfer of one file does not count
toward the next file's timeout.
.TP
.B "-n, --newer"
(ZMODEM and YMODEM) Accept file if the destination file does not exist.
Otherwise, accept the file, overwriting the destination, if the
source file is newer than the destination file.
.TP
.B "-N, --newer-or-longer"
(ZMODEM and YMODEM) Accept file if the destination file does not exist.
Otherwise, accept the file, overwriting the destination, if the
source file is newer or longer than the destination file.
.TP
.B "-O, --disable-timeouts"
Disable read timeout handling code. This makes lrz hang if the
sender does not send any more, but increases performance (a bit)
and decreases system load (through reducing the number of system
calls by about 50 percent).

Use this option with care.
.TP
.B "--o-sync"
Open output files in synchronous write mode. This may be useful if you
experience errors due to lost interrupts if update (or bdflush or
whoever this daemon is called on your system) writes the buffers to the
disk.

This option is ignored and a warning is printed if your systems 
doesn't support O_SYNC.
.TP
.B "-p, --protect"
(ZMODEM) Protect: skip file if destination file exists.
.TP
.B "-q, --quiet"
Quiet suppresses verbosity.
.TP
.B "-r, --resume"
Crash recovery mode. lrz tries to resume interrupted file transfers.
.TP
.B "-R, --restricted"
Enter the very restricted mode. lrz will not create directories or files
with a leading dot, and will not overwrite any existing file.
This option always enables the very restricted mode, even if it was
downgraded before (e.g. by combining it with the
.B ZMODEM_RESTRICTED
environment variable).

See
.B SECURITY
for the security model and the description of the restricted levels.
.TP
.B "--keep-incomplete"
Keep partially received files when a transfer fails with an error.
The default is to remove incomplete files; this option is the opt-in
for keeping them, e.g. to
.B --resume
the transfer later, or when receiving on a private system where the
partial data is worth more than the disk space. Interrupted
transfers (Ctrl-C, modem hangup) always keep the partial file.
.TP
.B "-s HH:MM, --stop-at HH:MM"
Stop transmission at
.B HH
hours,
.B MM
minutes. Another variant, using
.B +N
instead of
.B HH:MM,
stops transmission in
.B N
seconds. The deadline is tracked with a one-second granularity because
of the way the check is implemented (the
.B N
argument is parsed before any data flows and compared against integer
seconds afterwards); the transfer stops during the second after the
deadline is reached, so a transfer stops within about
.B N+1
to
.B N+2
seconds.
.TP
.B "--syslog[=off]"
turn syslogging on or off. Syslog is on by default.
.TP
.B "-t TIM, --timeout TIM"
Change timeout to
.I TIM
tenths of seconds. This is ignored if timeout handling is turned of
through the 
.B O 
option.
.TP
.B "-U, --unrestrict"
Removed. Security-relevant options cannot be turned off; see
.B SECURITY
and the
.B HISTORICAL NOTES
section. Use
.B --keep-incomplete
if you want failed transfers to leave the partial file in place.
.TP
.B "--version"
prints out version number.
.TP
.B "-v, --verbose"
Verbose output to stderr. With one
.BR v ,
a summary (file names, transfer result, error events) is printed. With two
.BR v s,
progress reports are also shown. More
.BR v s
are accepted but have no effect. Since version 0.13 detailed protocol debugging 
is available with 
.B \-\-debug
instead.
.TP
.B "\-\-debug=MODULES"
Enable debug output for the comma separated list of MODULES on stderr.
Known modules: "protocol" (ZMODEM frame state machine), "readline" (line
reader: select/read calls, timeouts, hex dump), "transfer" (file transfer
state machine), "windowhandling" (adaptive block length and window
logic, ZMODEM sender only), "tty" (terminal mode changes). The special
name "all" enables all.
.TP
.B "-wN, --windowsize N"
Set window size to N.
.TP
.B "-X, --xmodem"
use XMODEM protocol.
.TP
.B "-y, --overwrite"
Yes, clobber any existing files with the same name.
(Ignored in XMODEM mode, where lrz always writes to the given pathname.)
.TP
.B "--ymodem"
use YMODEM protocol.
.TP
.B "-Z, --zmodem"
use ZMODEM protocol.
.PD
.ne 6
.SH SECURITY
Contrary to the original ZMODEM lrz defaults to restricted mode.
Security-relevant options can be enabled but never disabled: restricted
mode cannot be turned off, and the very restricted mode cannot be
downgraded.

Remote command execution is always denied, in every mode.

lrz has two restricted levels:

.TP
.B restricted (the default)
lrz will not accept absolute pathnames or references to a parent
directory, will not modify an existing file, and removes any files
received in error (see
.B --keep-incomplete
for an exception).

.TP
.B very restricted
Additionally disables creation of subdirectories and files with a
leading dot, and refuses to overwrite existing files instead of
skipping them. Entered by setting the environment variable
.B ZMODEM_RESTRICTED
or giving the
.B R
option — in either case, and regardless of the order in which
options are given.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
lrz uses the following environment variables:
.TP
.B SHELL
lrz recognizes a restricted shell if this variable includes
.I "rsh"
or
.I "rksh"
\.
.TP
.B ZMODEM_RESTRICTED
lrz enters the very restricted mode if the variable is set.
Giving the
.B R
option also enables the very restricted mode.

.SH NOTES
Sending serial data to timesharing minicomputers
at sustained high speeds
has been known to cause lockups, system halts, kernel panics,
and occasional antisocial behaviour.
When experimenting with high speed input to a
system, consider rebooting the system
if the file transfers are not successful,
especially if the personality of the system appears altered.

The Unix "ulimit" parameter must be set high enough
to permit large file transfers.

The TTY input buffering on some systems may not allow long blocks
or streaming input at high speed.
You should suspect this problem when you
can't send data to the Unix system at high speeds using ZMODEM,
YMODEM-1k or XMODEM-1k,
when YMODEM with 128 byte blocks works properly.
If the system's tty line handling is really broken, the serial port
or the entire system may not survive the onslaught of long bursts
of high speed data.

32 bit CRC code courtesy Gary S. Brown.
Directory creation code from John Gilmore's PD TAR program.
.SH BUGS
Programs that do not properly implement the specified file transfer protocol
may cause
.I lrz
to "hang" the port for a minute or two.
Every reported instance of this problem has been corrected by using
a program with a correct implementation
of the specified protocol.

Many programs claiming to support YMODEM only support XMODEM with 1k blocks,
and they often don't get that quite right.

Pathnames are restricted to 127 characters.
In XMODEM single file mode, the pathname given on the command line
is still processed as described above.
The file management options (\-+, \-E, \-H, \-n, \-N, \-p, \-y)
are ZMODEM features (with partial YMODEM support); they are ignored
in XMODEM mode. XMODEM always writes to the pathname given on the
command line \- the restricted mode checks still apply.

The ASCII option\'s CR/LF to NL translation merely deletes CR\'s.
.SH "ZMODEM CAPABILITIES"
.I Rz
supports incoming ZMODEM binary (-b), ASCII (-a),
protect (-p),
clobber (-y),
and append (-+)
requests.
The default is protect (-p) and binary (-b).

The Unix versions support ZMODEM command execution.
.SH CAVEATS
The ZMODEM protocol uses 32-bit fields for file positions, imposing a
hard limit of 4 GiB on transfer size.
Furthermore, many ZMODEM implementations derived from the original
Omen Technology codebase (including
.IR crzsz ,
.IR rzsz ,
.IR lrzsz older than version 0.13,
and other 1990s variants) and also at least some independent
implementation use signed
.I long
internally, which on 32-bit platforms limits correct operation to
files below 2 GiB.
.B lrz
itself handles positions up to 4 GiB correctly, but interoperation
with older or 32-bit senders may fail for files larger than 2 GiB.
.SH "HISTORICAL NOTES"
The following options have been removed and are no longer functional.
Using them prints an error message and exits.
.TP
.B "-C, --allow-commands"
Allowed remote command execution via ZMODEM.
Removed because of weak security: it relied on the parent process
to only set this option when the remote user is perfectly trustworthy.
.TP
.B "-S, --timesync"
Time synchronization.
Removed because no other still-available implementation supported it,
and setting the local time would have required root privileges.
.TP
.B "-U, --unrestrict"
Turned off restricted mode (impossible under a restricted shell).
Removed because security-relevant options cannot be turned off:
whoever can pass \-U can equally well drop it from the command line,
so the option provided no protection — only a trap. The one
legitimate use, keeping partially received files on error, lives on
as the
.B --keep-incomplete
option. See
.BR SECURITY .
.TP
.B PUBDIR
The
.B PUBDIR
environment variable and the associated public directory feature
(restricting file transfers to
.IR PUBDIR ,
usually
.IR /var/spool/uucppublic )
have been removed.
.TP
.B "-B auto"
The
.B auto
argument to
.B -B / --bufsize
(formerly buffering the entire file in memory) has been removed.
No performance benefit was observed on modern systems.
